Body:
Fix-site update after reading the v2 sources, so the scope is unambiguous when someone picks this up.

**The pairing is one CSS rule.** `packages/session-ui/src/components/message-part.css:1472-1477` is the workspace-session bubble:

```
:root body [data-workspace-session] [data-component="user-message"] [data-slot="user-message-text"] {
  --user-message-mention-prefix-color: var(--v2-blue-300);
  background: var(--v2-background-bg-accent);
  color: var(--v2-text-text-contrast);
}
```

**The drift is visible right below it.** The local-session rules at `:1479-1497` are per-color-scheme and pass comfortably - light `--v2-blue-100` on `--v2-blue-700` is 7.03:1, dark `--v2-blue-1200` on `--v2-blue-300` is 11.53:1. The workspace rule is the only one of the three that is not scheme-branched and the only one that pairs a fixed semantic foreground with a theme-derived surface.

**Why the default theme escapes and One Dark Pro does not.** `oc-2` overrides `v2-blue-600` explicitly, so `bg-accent` stays `#3b5cf6` in both schemes and white on it is 5.19:1. One Dark Pro ships only a palette, so `bg-accent` is derived from `palette.primary` (`resolve.ts:112`) and its dark value lands at `#3eacff`, where - as noted before - even pure white only reaches 2.46:1. Any custom theme with a light `primary` has the same problem.

**There is an existing e2e regression that pins the current pairing**, so it has to move with the fix: `packages/app/e2e/regression/workspace-accent.spec.ts:78-87` asserts exactly these two tokens for the accent scenarios and runs them under both light and dark.

Two shapes, and I would rather a maintainer pick:

1. **Derive it, the way icons already do.** The icon half of this role is contrast-picked against the surface it sits on (`theme/v2/foreground.ts:57`, `pickGrey(primitives, bgContrast, 7, 100)`); the text half is a static `ref("v2-grey-100")` in `theme/v2/mapping.ts` (`:48` light, `:116` dark). Following `AGENTS.md`, the accent-surface foreground is a semantic role the theme does not currently expose, so this means adding it across schema, defaults, resolution and types, resolving it contrast-aware against `bg-accent`, and using it here - plus verifying the built-in light and dark defaults and the custom-theme fallback, since it changes the public token surface.
2. **Reuse the precedent in the same file**: give the workspace bubble the same per-scheme treatment its two neighbours already have, keeping the accent background that distinguishes a workspace session and choosing the dark-scheme foreground so it clears the threshold on a bright accent.

(1) fixes every theme; (2) is a much smaller diff but leaves the next bright-accent theme to hit the same wall. I am happy to do either - say which and I will open the PR.

Do you use shell or read permission = deny?

Example:
{
  "action": "shell",
  "resource": "*",
  "effect": "deny"
},
{
  "action": "read",
  "resource": "*",
  "effect": "deny"
}

In my case, changing both from "deny" to "allow" fixes the bug.
